en user and item latent vectors rather to an item-mean plus neighbor-weighted sum.\\n\\n8) Baselines (global mean and user mean predictions; report RMSE/MAE): Not implemented. No explicit baselines; only MF-based predictions and RMSE on validation. Better to report baseline metrics as separate lines (global mean, user mean) and MAE.\\n\\n9) Evaluation metrics (RMSE, MAE; coverage; runtime): Partially satisfied. The code reports RMSE on training/validation and a runtime estimate. It does not compute MAE or prediction coverage, and does not report a dedicated test-set RMSE/MAE pair. Recommend: compute RMSE and MAE for both baselines and model, report test coverage (fraction of test cases with a valid prediction) and a clear runtime breakdown.\\n\\n10) Hyperparameters (k, similarity metric; validation): Not satisfied. No k or metric=cosine/Pearson exposed as hyperparameters; defaults hard-coded for MF; validation of hyperparameters is absent. Recommend: expose k, similarity_metric as arguments, validate them (k > 0, metric in \{cosine, pearson\}).\\n\\n11) Reproducibility and determinism: Partially satisfied. Seeds are set for train/val split and initialization, which helps reproducibility. However there is an inner random shuffle in each epoch with a different seed (epoch) which could marginally affect results. Recommend: fix a single seed for all random operations or use deterministic shuffles controlled by the seed, and document seed usage clearly.\\n\\n12) Robustness and efficiency (sparse formats; division by zero; error handling): Partially satisfied. Uses pandas without sparse structures; risk of slow training for large datasets; no explicit guards against division-by-zero or missing data across various operations. Recommend: rely on scipy.sparse matrices for rating storage and ensure safe handling when neighbors are missing.\\n\\n13) Modularity and outputs (modules; concise summary; sample Top-5 recs; optional CSV): Partially satisfied. Code is functionally organized but not fully modularized into clear modules (load/preprocess/sim/predict/eval). It should print a concise summary and sample Top-5 recommendations for a random test user, and support optional CSV saving. Recommend: refactor into dedicated modules with clear interfaces and add a final summary plus a Top-5 sample.\\n\\nOverall score: 28%. \\n\\nKey improvement plan:\\n- Implement the specified item-based CF pipeline (data -> preprocessing with filters -> build sparse user-item matrix -> train/test split with 80/20 determinism -> compute item-item cosine similarity on training set with per-item centering and store per-item means -> compute top-k neighbors -> prediction rule with item_mean_i + weighted sum over user ratings on neighboring items, fallback to global mean when no neighbors exist).\\n- Add global and user baselines with RMSE/MAE reporting; compute coverage and runtimes.\\n- Expose hyperparameters (k, similarity_metric) with validation and clear error messages.\\n- Ensure full reproducibility (single seed for all randomness, deterministic operations).\\n- Improve robustness with sparse matrix operations, guard against empty neighbor sets, and provide error handling.\\n- Refactor into a clean, modular API and provide outputs such as a concise summary and a Top-5 sample for a test user; allow optional CSV export.', is_satisfactory=False)\
